Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from the story
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. STONE CELL - NIGHT

A damp, windowless room lit by a single flickering gas lamp. NARRATOR (30s, gaunt, wearing a disheveled 19th-century waistcoat) paces the stone floor. His eyes are wide, manic, yet he forces a chillingly calm smile.

(SOUND: water dripping steadily from the ceiling)

NARRATOR
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad? The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them.

He stops pacing and leans in close, speaking to an unseen listener in the shadows.

NARRATOR
Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell. How, then, am I mad? Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

(MUSIC: a low, unsettling cello drone begins)

NARRATOR
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night. Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man.

INT. PARLOR - DAY

Sunlight streams through lace curtains. OLD MAN (70s, frail, white hair, wearing a heavy dressing gown) sits in a high-backed chair. 

(SOUND: a porcelain teacup clinking against a saucer)

Narrator, looking perfectly composed and kind, pours tea for the Old Man. The Old Man looks up. One of his eyes is normal; the other is a pale blue vulture eye, covered with a thick, milky film.

Narrator’s hand trembles slightly as the eye fixes on him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

Narrator forces a warm smile and hands the Old Man the tea.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. STONE CELL - NIGHT

Narrator sits on a wooden cot, his gaunt face half-swallowed by the shadows. He taps his temple knowingly.

(SOUND: chains rattling faintly in the distance)

NARRATOR
Now this is the point. You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

INT. HALLWAY - NIGHT

Pitch black. Narrator stands before a heavy oak door. He holds a dark tin lantern, completely closed so no light escapes. 

(SOUND: a brass latch clicking with agonizing slowness)

He turns the latch and pushes the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him. And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The room is suffocatingly dark. The Old Man sleeps soundly in a large wooden bed. 

Narrator’s head slowly, imperceptibly, inches through the doorway. 

(SOUND: hinges creaking, barely a whisper)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It took me an hour to place my whole head within the opening so far that I could see him as he lay upon his bed. Ha!—would a madman have been so wise as this?

Narrator carefully undoes the latch of the lantern. A single, razor-thin ray of light shoots out, landing directly on the Old Man's face. 

The vulture eye is closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - DAY

Morning light. The Old Man sits up in bed. Narrator strides into the room, his posture bold and cheerful.

(SOUND: heavy, confident footsteps on the floorboards)

NARRATOR
Good morning to you! And how have you passed the night?

The Old Man smiles, suspecting nothing.

INT. STONE CELL - NIGHT

Narrator stands abruptly from the cot, his disheveled waistcoat heaving with his breath. He grips the iron bars of the cell door.

(MUSIC: the cello drone returns, faster, more erratic)

NARRATOR
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The eighth night. Total darkness. Narrator’s head is already inside the room. He holds the closed lantern. 

His thumb rests on the tin fastening. It slips.

(SOUND: a sharp, metallic clink)

The Old Man springs up in bed, his silhouette rigid against the dark.

OLD MAN
Who’s there?

Narrator freezes. He does not breathe. 

(SOUND: wind howling faintly in the chimney)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down. He was still sitting up in the bed listening.

A long, agonizing silence. Then, from the bed, a sound.

(SOUND: a low, stifled groan of mortal terror)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew the sound well. Many a night, just at midnight, when all the world slept, it has welled up from my own bosom. I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

Narrator’s fingers delicately adjust the lantern. He opens a microscopic crevice. 

A single dim ray, like the thread of a spider, shoots across the pitch-black room. It falls full upon the vulture eye.

It is open. Wide, wide open. 

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick sound begins—like a watch enveloped in cotton)

Narrator’s own eyes widen in fury. He stares down the beam of light at the hideous blue veil.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man’s heart. It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

The heartbeat grows louder. Quicker. 

(SOUND: the heartbeat booms, vibrating the room)

Narrator grips the lantern, his knuckles white. He trembles, sweating profusely. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man’s terror must have been extreme! It grew louder, I say, louder every moment! And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man’s hour had come!

Narrator throws open the lantern. The room is flooded with harsh light. 

With a loud yell, Narrator leaps into the room.

(SOUND: a single, piercing shriek from the Old Man)

Narrator tackles the frail Old Man to the floor. He grabs the heavy mattress and wooden bedframe, hauling them violently over the struggling body.

(SOUND: a heavy, crushing thud)

Narrator sits atop the overturned bed, smiling gaily, panting. 

Beneath the mattress, the muffled heartbeat continues. 

(SOUND: muffled heartbeat, slowing down... slowing... stopping)

Narrator tosses the bed aside. He places his hand upon the Old Man's chest. Dead. 

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - LATER

The room is lit by the lantern. Narrator has pulled up three heavy wooden planks from the floor. 

He works hastily, in silence, depositing unseen, heavy bundles into the dark space between the scantlings. 

(SOUND: dull thuds of weight settling on wood)

He replaces the boards. He wipes the wood with a rag, inspecting it closely. Perfectly seamless. No blood. He chuckles softly.

INT. FOYER - NIGHT

A grandfather clock chimes four times. It is still dark outside.

(SOUND: heavy, authoritative knocking at the street door)

Narrator, looking perfectly calm, walks down the stairs and opens the door. 

Three men stand on the porch, wearing 19th-century police uniforms: OFFICER REYNOLDS (mustached, polite), OFFICER SMITH (tall, imposing), and OFFICER JONES (stout).

REYNOLDS
Apologies for the hour, sir. A shriek was heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play has been aroused. We have been deputed to search the premises.

Narrator smiles warmly, stepping aside to let them in.

NARRATOR
You are most welcome, gentlemen. The shriek, I confess, was my own in a dream. The old man is absent in the country. Please, search well.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Narrator leads Reynolds, Smith, and Jones into the bedroom. The officers inspect the walls, the closet, the undisturbed treasures. 

(SOUND: heavy police boots walking directly over the hidden floorboards)

Narrator brings three wooden chairs into the center of the room. 

NARRATOR
Rest from your fatigues, gentlemen. 

He grabs a fourth chair and places it with wild audacity upon the exact spot beneath which the corpse reposes. He sits, crossing his legs.

Reynolds sits opposite him, putting away his notebook.

REYNOLDS
Well, everything appears to be in perfect order. A quiet night, after all.

The officers relax. They smile and chat silently among themselves. Narrator answers cheerily. 

But Narrator’s smile begins to strain. He pales. He rubs his ear.

(SOUND: a faint, low, dull, quick sound begins—like a watch enveloped in cotton)

Narrator glances at the officers. They don't seem to hear it. He leans forward, talking faster, his voice heightened.

NARRATOR
Yes, the country air does him well! He plans to stay the season!

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows louder, more distinct)

Narrator gasps for breath. He stands up, pacing the floor to and fro with heavy strides. He gestures violently as he speaks.

NARRATOR
It is a trifle, really! A mere trifle of a dream!

The officers continue to chat pleasantly, smiling at him. 

(SOUND: the heartbeat is deafening now, a relentless, booming rhythm)

Narrator foams at the mouth. He swears under his breath. He grabs his chair and swings it, grating the wooden legs violently against the floorboards.

(SOUND: harsh, screeching wood scraping over the booming heartbeat)

The officers look at him, still smiling their polite, hypocritical smiles. 

Narrator clutches his head, his eyes bulging. The noise is unbearable. 

NARRATOR
Villains!

The officers startle, half-rising from their chairs.

NARRATOR
Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

INT. STONE CELL - NIGHT

Narrator is on his knees on the damp stone floor, his disheveled waistcoat torn open. He screams into the empty, flickering shadows, clawing at his own ears.

(SOUND: the booming heartbeat echoes off the stone walls, reaching a fever pitch)

NARRATOR
Louder! Louder! Louder!

(SOUND: the heartbeat abruptly stops)

Silence. Narrator collapses forward, sobbing into the cold stone.

(MUSIC: a final, mournful cello note fades into the dark)
